cussion","anchor":"discussion","type":"heading","level":2},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"With an admissible heuristic (never more than the true distance) the path is a shortest"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"one, even when the heuristic is not consistent, since a vertex whose distance improves after"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"it was expanded is expanded again. With a consistent one ("},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"h(u) <= w(u→v) + h(v)"},{"type":"text","text":") no vertex"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"is expanded twice. A heuristic of zero is Dijkstra’s algorithm. The estimate of "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"target"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"itself is taken as zero, so a negative one there (still admissible) cannot end the search"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"early, and "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"heuristic"},{"type":"text","text":" is not called for it."}]},{"style":"note","name":"Precondition","type":"aside","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"As for "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"dijkstraShortestPath(from:to:weight:)"},{"type":"text","text":"; no estimate is NaN."}]}]}],"kind":"content"}],"references":{"doc://GraphProtocols/documentation/GraphProtocols/DirectedGraph/Vertex":{"fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"associatedtype"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"Vertex"},{"kind":"text","text":" : "},{"preciseIdentifier":"s:SH","kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"Hashable"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"keyword","text":"where"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"Self"},{"kind":"text","text":"."},{"preciseIdentifier":"s:14GraphProtocols08DirectedA0P6VertexQa","kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"Vertex"},{"kind":"text","text":" == "},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"Self"},{"kind":"text","text":"."},{"preciseIdentifier":"s:14GraphProtocols0